  "id": "Compound::DB01595 - Gene::2557",
  "verbalization": "Nitrazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrd, Gabrq, and Gabrp, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These subunits are involved in the activation of Gaba-a receptor activity, which is a critical step in the modulation of neuronal excitability. The evidence suggests that Nitrazepam exerts its effects by interacting with the Gaba-a receptor complex, potentially modulating its function and contributing to the observed effects on neuronal activity. The consistent binding patterns across different subunits indicate a general interaction with the Gaba-a receptor complex, which is a well-documented mechanism in the context of GABAergic neurotransmission. The integrated evidence supports a mechanism where Nitrazepam influences Gaba-a receptor activity, thereby affecting neuronal function and possibly contributing to its therapeutic or pharmacological effects.",
